## 1. Step 1 — the three statements the paper actually makes

The paper contains **three distinct statements**, not two. The author's S1 and S2 map onto
two of them (and those two are two halves of *one* statement). The third — the one the
author did **not** recall — is the one that maps most dangerously onto our pipeline.

### P1 — the SELECTION DENOMINATOR breaks separability (single-event statement)

**[LIT] §2.3, paragraph following Eq. (30):**

> "However, this is a function of the true values of the galaxy redshifts. This dependence
> of the denominator of the GW likelihood on {z_g} **breaks the separability of the integrals
> that we exploited above, unless the true redshifts of the galaxies are perfectly known.**
> In this case, it can be seen that the full hierarchical likelihood reduces to Eq. 15. In
> practice, however, we will not know the true redshifts of the galaxies. The detection
> probability is effectively an average of the galaxy redshift distribution over the whole
> volume within which GW sources can be observed. **If that volume is sufficiently large**,
> as is the case for current GW detectors, then the average galaxy redshift will be
> approximately uniform in comoving volume, and so the dependence of the detection probability
> on the actual galaxy redshifts will be relatively weak and so this term can be factored out,
> reducing the result to the simpler expression used in this paper."

Note what this is and is not:
- It is a **single-event** statement. It needs no second event and no shared host.
- The escape clause is a **large-VOLUME / uniform-in-comoving-volume** argument about the
  *detection* volume, not a large-count argument about the candidate list.
- Its precondition for exactness is **perfect galaxy redshifts**.

**[LIT] §2.3, Eq. (30)** (the object in question):
> `p_det(H_0, {z_g}) = (1/N_gal) Σ_{j=1}^{N_gal} ∫_{-∞}^{d_L^thr} L_GW(x | d_L(z_g^j, H_0)) dx
>  = (1/N_gal) Σ_{j=1}^{N_gal} p_det^GW(d_L(z_g^j, H_0))`

### P2 — the MULTI-EVENT cross-terms (= the author's S1)

**[LIT] §2.3, next paragraph (immediately after P1), leading to Eq. (31):**

> "**The second correction arises when considering multiple GW observations. The likelihood
> is the product of likelihoods for each GW observation, but each one of those likelihoods
> includes the sum over galaxies. This will introduce cross terms which are not simply the
> product of the individually-marginalized likelihoods, but marginals of the product of the
> likelihoods over the true redshift of the galaxies.** These terms represent corrections for
> the case when multiple GW events are observed from the same galaxy. In practice, **these
> corrections are small, as there are typically 1/N_gal times fewer of them than the dominant
> independent-host terms. These terms will only become important once a significant number of
> sources in the catalogue share a host.** With typical merger rates of a few tens per Gpc^3
> per year and approximately one galaxy per cubic megaparsec, the typical spacing of mergers
> in any given galaxy is millions of years, so we will essentially never be in a regime where
> these corrections matter.[fn 4] **Once again, we emphasise that these terms only arise when
> the galaxy redshift measurements are imperfect. When galaxy redshifts are known perfectly,
> Eq. 15 is still exact when analysing many GW sources.**"

(Footnote 4 is the Adams joke: "Space is big. Really big…")

**[LIT] §2.3, Eq. (31)** — the exact, non-factorizing form:
> `p(H_0 | {x}) ∝ p(H_0) ∫ d{z_g} [ Π_{j=1}^{N_obs} L_GW(x_j | {z_g}, H_0) / p_det(H_0, {z_g}) ]
>  × [ Π_{k=1}^{N_gal} L_red(ẑ_g^k | z_g^k) ] p_bg({z_g})`
> with `L_GW(x_j | {z_g}, H_0) = (1/N_gal) Σ_{i=1}^{N_gal} L_GW(x_j | d_L(z_g^i, H_0))`.

**[LIT] §2.3, closing:**
> "This expression involves multiplication of a sum over galaxies for each GW event, followed
> by an integration over all the unknown true galaxy redshifts. This is extremely
> computationally expensive, which is why it is better to use the standard approximate
> expression, especially since the latter is expected to be accurate in any application to
> real data."

### P3 — the large-N_gal validity condition and its empirical demonstration (= the author's S2)

**[LIT] §3.3 "The one galaxy limit", opening sentence:**

> "As described in Section 2.3, **the standard analysis makes some approximations that are
> only valid in the limit that the gravitational wave detection volume contains a sufficiently
> large number of galaxies.** To illustrate this, we consider here an extreme and unrealistic
> example in which the galaxy catalogue contains only one galaxy, the redshift of which is
> known imperfectly from electromagnetic observations."

**[LIT] §3.3, the measured bias:**
> "In Figure 8 we show example posteriors for catalogues generated with H_0 = 70 km s^-1 Mpc^-1,
> assuming the uncertainty in the EM redshift measurement is δz/z = 3% or 0.3%. **We see that
> with a 3% error, the posterior is shifted significantly to the right and shows a large bias.**
> For the smaller redshift uncertainty the posterior appears to be unbiased, but in fact a
> small bias is still present on average. This is revealed by constructing a PP-plot over 100
> random realisations of the Universe. The PP-plot is shown in Figure 9 and **there is a clear
> and significant departure from the diagonal, even when δz/z = 0.3%.**"

**[LIT] §3.3, the attribution — this is the load-bearing sentence:**
> "**These biases arise from the fact that in the standard analysis the approximation has been
> used that the true redshift of the galaxy can be marginalised out of the likelihood separately
> for each GW event.** To carry out a consistent analysis we must use the full likelihood given
> in Eq. (31). In the case that there is only one galaxy, with unknown true redshift z and
> observed redshift ẑ, this simplifies to [Eq. **(33)**]:
> `p(H_0|{x}) ∝ p(H_0) ∫ dz [ Π_{j=1}^{N_obs} L_GW(x_j|d_L(z,H_0)) / p_det(H_0,z) ] × [L_red(ẑ|z)] p(z)`"

**[LIT] §3.3, the fix and the perfect-z limit:**
> "We see that the full likelihood broadens the posterior, making it consistent with the true
> value even in the case of large redshift uncertainties, and it generates a pp-plot that is
> perfectly consistent with the expected diagonal line. The figures also show results computed
> in the perfect-redshift measurement limit, δz/z = 0%. **In this limit the full and approximate
> likelihoods are exactly equivalent and so we recover unbiased results using either likelihood.**"

**[LIT] §3.3, the practical warning:**
> "**Using galaxy catalogues that are too sparse can lead to biases** because of the inconsistency
> in the standard analysis that becomes apparent in this limit. However, these are not indicative
> of a fundamental problem in the analysis, and can be avoided by using the full likelihood of
> Eq. (31)."

**[LIT] §3.2 (the operating rule the paper adopts for its own runs):**
> "In the limit that we have N_GW ≥ N_gal, **the simplified framework cannot be used**[fn 6] …
> otherwise, there could be a bias (see Sec. 3.3) for more details."
> (fn 6: "This an extremely unrealistic limit where we assume that multiple GW sources are hosted
> in the same galaxy.")

**[LIT] §4.2 "Inconsistency 5, simulating dark siren events along the same line of sight"** —
the *opposite-sign* companion, and the only place the paper gives a **low-H_0** direction for
this mechanism:
> "The statistical method used in TH21 is for the high-galaxy density regime, but this is
> inconsistent with the generation of data from D_11 and D_21, where it is possible for multiple
> GW events to be drawn from the same low-redshift host galaxy. … However, in our own analyses,
> this produced a noticeable bias less frequently than the other potential sources of bias we
> explored. Moreover, **we find that a low-H_0 bias will arise if there are low-redshift
> overdensities along the line of sight and the full likelihood analysis is not used.** When
> simulating multiple events along the same line of sight, if there is an overdensity of
> galaxies in that direction, the H_0 posterior is expected to display a peak at an H_0 lower
> than the input value, that corresponds to the redshift of the overdensity … Especially when
> using the light cones with fewer galaxies, there are very few galaxies at the redshift of
> interest z ≲ 0.3, so we are sensitive to the small number statistics of a peak in the redshift
> distribution … **the presence of the same galaxies across multiple GW events needs to be taken
> into account with the full likelihood analysis so as not to incur in a bias.**"

### Statements that DO **NOT** exist in the paper (checked, negative results)

**[LIT]** Searched the full text for each; report explicitly as absent:

- **No** statement about correlations induced by **shared population hyperparameters** across
  events. (The paper's hyperparameter is H_0 alone; rate evolution is deliberately switched off
  — §2.1: "we will neglect the rate term in Eq. 5 in order to align more closely with the
  approach taken in TH21".)
- **No** statement about **catalogue-realization** correlations (a shared *noise* realization of
  the catalogue inducing correlated per-event likelihoods). The paper's only shared-catalogue
  correlation is the shared **latent true redshifts** {z_g} — a marginalization coupling, not a
  realization coupling.
- **No** statement about correlations from a **shared selection/injection-pool estimate**.
  (P1 is the nearest thing, and it concerns the *exact* p_det's dependence on the latent {z_g},
  not on a Monte-Carlo estimate of it.)
- The paper's **"double counting"** (§4.2 Inconsistency 1) is **NOT** about double-counting hosts
  across events. **[LIT] §4.2:** "since the sample of MICEcat galaxies is already distributed as
  Eq. 13, this procedure effectively introduces a 'double counting' problem. In other words, if
  the distribution of galaxies follows a z^2 profile, the distribution of GW events will follow a
  z^4 profile. … **this type of mismatch usually results in a bias towards lower values of H_0.**"
  That is a generator/estimator prior mismatch — a different animal, and one this project already
  quotes (`docs/BIAS_RESOLUTION_ATTEMPTS_REPORT.md:266`).
- No adjacent-canon check was needed: both S1 and S2 are present in this paper. (Gray et al. 2020
  arXiv:1908.06050 / Mandel, Farr & Gair 2019 arXiv:1809.02063 were **not** re-read this session;
  any claim about them here would be untagged and is therefore not made. See Refute-by on **H-6**.)

---

## 2. S1 vs S2 — the disentanglement

**Verdict: the author is not conflating two unrelated things — S1 and S2 are the two ends of ONE
statement. But the author's recollection is missing a genuinely distinct THIRD statement (P1),
and that omission is the interesting part.**

| author's recollection | paper statement | status |
|---|---|---|
| **S1** "the per-detection-independent integral is an approximation; the full form does not factorize and carries correlations between detections" | **P2** (§2.3 after Eq. 30, Eq. 31) | **CORRECT, verbatim.** |
| **S2** "some approximation only holds when the number of possible hosts is LARGE" | **P3** (§3.3 opening; §3.2 fn 6; §4.2 Inconsistency 5) | **CORRECT, verbatim.** |
| (not recalled) | **P1** (§2.3 after Eq. 30) — the *selection denominator* breaks separability unless galaxy z is perfect; rescued by a **large-detection-volume** argument | **DISTINCT third statement.** |

**S1 and S2 are the same object.** P3's "sufficiently large number of galaxies" is precisely the
condition under which P2's `1/N_gal` suppression of the cross-terms holds. §3.3 says so in one
sentence — the one-galaxy bias "arise[s] from the fact that … the true redshift of the galaxy can
be marginalised out of the likelihood **separately for each GW event**". So: *the factorization
approximation (S1) is the approximation whose validity requires large N_gal (S2).* They are not
two independent claims to be checked separately.

**Three consequences that matter for us, all read directly off the quotes:**

1. **The mechanism requires imperfect galaxy redshifts. Full stop.** [LIT] §2.3: "these terms
   only arise when the galaxy redshift measurements are imperfect. When galaxy redshifts are
   known perfectly, Eq. 15 is still exact when analysing many GW sources." [LIT] §3.3: at
   δz/z = 0% "the full and approximate likelihoods are exactly equivalent".
2. **The mechanism requires the SAME galaxies to be summed over by MORE THAN ONE event.** "These
   terms will only become important once a significant number of sources in the catalogue share a
   host"; "the presence of the same galaxies across multiple GW events". A single event with few
   candidates is **not** in the failure regime — the §3.3 demonstration puts **200 GW events on
   one galaxy**. Few-hosts-per-event, with no sharing, does not trigger P2/P3.
3. **The sign is regime-dependent, and the paper gives both.** §3.3 (one shared galaxy,
   δz/z = 3%): **HIGH** ("shifted significantly to the right"). §4.2 Inconsistency 5 (shared line
   of sight with a low-z overdensity): **LOW**. Anyone claiming a sign for our pipeline must name
   which regime they are in.

**P1 is the statement with no shared-host precondition.** It says the *selection denominator*,
which genuinely depends on the latent galaxy field, is factored out of the {z_g} integral on a
large-volume/uniform-comoving-volume argument. That is a modelling assumption our pipeline makes
everywhere and has never audited against this quote.

---

## 3. Step 2 — mapping onto our pipeline

### 3.1 Where we assume the per-event-independent form

**C-1 [LOCAL] — our joint posterior is exactly the factorized form.**
`master_thesis_code/bayesian_inference/posterior_combination.py:324`:
```python
joint_log = np.sum(log_likes, axis=0)
```
over an `(n_events, n_h_values)` array — i.e. `ln p(h|{x}) = Σ_i ln L_i(h) + const`, the paper's
Eq. (3)/(15) product, **not** Eq. (31). There is no {z_g} marginalization outside the per-event
loop anywhere in the codebase.
*Refute by:* grep the `--combine` path for any cross-event coupling term; if `np.sum(log_likes,
axis=0)` at `:324` is the only combination and no caller passes event-coupled likelihoods, C-1 stands.
(Note the same function's docstring at `:300-315` records that a `−N·log D(h)` term was removed
in the Tier-3 audit — the only historical cross-event object, and it was a selection correction,
not a P2 cross-term.)

**C-2 [LOCAL] — the per-event likelihood is a two-leg mixture; only one leg is galaxy-summed.**
`bayesian_statistics.py:3813` (`p_Di`) builds
`L_i = w_G · L_cat,i + (1 − w_G) · L_comp,i`, where `L_cat,i` sums `single_host_likelihood`
(`:4658`) over **that event's own candidate ball** returned by
`get_possible_hosts_from_ball_tree` (`:3649`; radius definition
`galaxy_catalogue/handler.py:558-618`, `2σ√λ_max(JΣJᵀ)`), and `L_comp,i` is a smooth
completeness-corrected integral carrying **no discrete galaxies at all**.
⇒ **P2/P3 can only enter through `L_cat`.** [INFER]
*Refute by:* show `L_comp` depends on any individual catalogue galaxy's observed redshift
(it depends on `f_k` — see C-7 — which is the one live doubt).

**C-3 [LOCAL] — the completion leg carries ~93% of the mixture weight, and the 2D catalogue leg
is identically zero for most events.** Measured from
`results/run_20260804_postfix/joint_r1/diagnostics/event_likelihoods.csv`, 1588 events at the
mid-grid h:
- `w_G` median = mean = **0.0708** ⇒ completion share **0.9292**
- `L_cat_with_bh == 0` for **61.8%** of events; `L_cat_no_bh == 0` for **31.0%**
(This reproduces the gate-(vii)/N-1 figures at the joint venue; the 81.5% figure is the idealized
venue.)
*Refute by:* recompute the same two columns at any other h on the grid; the numbers are h-stable
by construction of `w_G`.

**C-4 [LOCAL] — shared candidate galaxies between events are RARE but NOT zero.**
Computed from `results/run_20260804_postfix/joint_r1/diagnostics/prepared_cramer_rao_bounds.csv`
(1590 events) using the production ball radius formula (`handler.py:613-617`):
- ball radius (chord): median **1.22°**, p90 **3.65°**, max **15.98°**
- **event pairs with overlapping sky balls: 1620 of 1 263 255 (0.128%)**; **981/1590 events (61.7%)**
  have ≥1 sky-overlapping partner (median degree 1, p90 6, max 40)
- adding a 2σ `d_L`-window overlap requirement: **279 pairs (0.022%)**, touching **385 events (24.2%)**
This is an **upper bound** on P2-relevant sharing: it ignores the mass cut and the z-window, both of
which only shrink it.
*Refute by:* re-run with actual `catalog_index` sets per event (requires a diagnostics emit that does
not currently exist) — the exact overlap count is ≤ 279 pairs.

**C-5 [DOC/LOCAL] — the 1D low rail is present at the EXACT-z venue.**
`results/run_20260804_postfix/gate_vii/PREREGISTRATION_FROZEN_GFRAC.md:43` defines
"iiib = idealized (parent/exact-z); joint_r1 = realization r1 (delivered/observed)" **[DOC]**.
Reading the combined posteriors **[LOCAL]**: `iiib/combined_1d.json` → `map_h = 0.6`
(`map_posterior` 0.6653); `joint_r1/combined_1d.json` → `map_h = 0.6` (0.4708). Both rail at the
grid edge 0.600.
*Refute by:* show that the iiib venue's *estimator kernel* is not δ-like — i.e. that `L_red` is not
effectively a delta even though the catalogue is the exact-z parent. **This is the one open hole in
C-5 and it is cheap to close** (see §6, M-1).

**C-6 [LOCAL] — every selection object is precomputed ONCE and shared by all events.**
`bayesian_statistics.py:2982-3078` inside `evaluate()`: `precompute_completion_denominator` (2982),
`precompute_missing_completion_denominator` (2998), `precompute_global_catalog_selection`
(3005/3013/3045), `precompute_phi_marginal_survival` (3033), `precompute_phi_selection_integrals`
(3038), `compute_catalog_draw_weight_total` (3075), `precompute_completeness_population_volume`
(3078). All are consumed per-event via module globals; `completion_mass_factor_g` (`:4197`) reads
them. ⇒ our `D(h)`, `β_Ḡ`, `S̄_φ`, `Σ^4D`, `V_f` are treated as **exactly known functions of h**,
built from the injection pool — precisely the object P1 says is "a function of the true values of
the galaxy redshifts" and may only be factored out under a large-volume argument. [INFER]
*Refute by:* show any of these tables is re-derived per event conditional on that event's candidate
redshifts (it is not — they are module globals set before the event loop).

**C-7 [DOC] — `f_k` is pool-fed into the CATALOGUE leg.** RUNBOOK-8 §0 "Discovered facts" and
BIAS_HISTORY_LEDGER §4 item 15 (row #94): "under `volume_deconv`/`absolute_marginal` the
catalogue-leg host-z prior carries the completeness callable `f_k` built from the injection pool …
62–69% of `L_cat_no_bh` cells move under a pool substitution." ⇒ the one place in our code where a
**globally-estimated selection object enters a LOCAL, small-N_gal sum** — the exact intersection of
P1's escape clause (which needs a *large* volume) with a *ball-sized* volume. [INFER]
*Refute by:* the D1 A1/A2 arms already measured the *magnitude* of a pool substitution on `L_cat`;
re-read `results/run_20260805_d1/readout.json` for the h-slope (not just the level) of that movement.

### 3.2 Which of the paper's validity conditions we satisfy / violate

| paper condition | our 1D channel | our 2D (with-BH-mass) channel |
|---|---|---|
| **perfect galaxy redshifts** (P2/P3 exactness) | **VIOLATED at joint_r1** (GLADE photo-z, σ_z ≈ 0.035 ⇒ δz/z ≈ 7–35% over z ∈ [0.1, 0.5], vs the paper's 3% "large bias" case) — **SATISFIED at iiib** (exact-z parent) | same |
| **same galaxy summed by ≥2 events** (P2/P3 necessity) | **~SATISFIED for ≤24.2% of events / ≤0.022% of pairs** (C-4) | **WEAKER** — mass conditioning zeroes `L_cat` outright for 61.8% of events (C-3), so those events contribute no galaxy sum to share |
| **large N_gal in the sum** (P3) | ball-sized, not measured per-event here | **strongly reduced** — the mass factor concentrates weight and, for 61.8% of events, empties the ball |
| **large detection volume ⇒ p_det ≈ uniform-comoving** (P1 escape clause) | **SATISFIED by construction in `L_comp`** (uniform-in-comoving completion density) | same for `L_comp`; **QUESTIONABLE in `L_cat` via `f_k`** (C-7) |
| **mixture weight on the galaxy-summed leg** | 7.1% (C-3) | 7.1%, and zero for 61.8% of events |

**The author's S2 concern, stated precisely and then answered:** the 2D channel's mass conditioning
*does* shrink the effective host count, and dramatically — 61.8% of events have an identically-zero
2D catalogue leg. **But the paper's failure mode is not "few hosts"; it is "few hosts, shared across
events, with imperfect redshifts."** Shrinking a *per-event* candidate ball toward one galaxy, when
that galaxy is summed by exactly one event, keeps the paper's Eq. (31) factorization **exact** — the
one-galaxy integral separates trivially. The §3.3 bias appears only because 200 events share it.
Mass conditioning therefore pushes us *away* from, not toward, the P2 regime: it reduces the number
of event pairs that can share a candidate. [INFER]

### 3.3 Could the neglected correlations produce the observed signatures?

**H-1 (1D low rail at 0.600, both venues) — REFUTED on the paper's own stated condition.**
Mechanism argument: P2/P3 corrections are *identically absent* when galaxy redshifts are perfect
([LIT] §2.3 "When galaxy redshifts are known perfectly, Eq. 15 is still exact when analysing many GW
sources"; §3.3 "In this limit the full and approximate likelihoods are exactly equivalent"). The
iiib venue is the exact-z parent catalogue **[DOC]** and it rails at 0.600 **[LOCAL, C-5]**. A
mechanism that vanishes at exact z cannot own a rail that persists at exact z.
Secondary, independent: even where it does not vanish, the effect is bounded by
(mixture weight 0.071) × (fraction of events with a shared candidate ≤ 0.242) × (1/N_shared),
i.e. sub-percent on the per-event log-likelihood — orders of magnitude short of a hard rail that
survives −1.1…−1.6 nats to the next grid point (ledger #93). [INFER]
*Refute by:* **M-1** below — demonstrate the iiib estimator's host-z kernel is not δ-like. If iiib's
kernel carries a finite σ_z, H-1's refutation collapses and this becomes a live candidate.

**H-2 (+0.05–0.07 2D displacement) — DISTINGUISHABLE / does not fit.**
Sign: the only 2D-relevant P2 regime the paper measures (§3.3, shared galaxy, imperfect z) biases
**HIGH**, which is the right direction. But the carrier is already identified: the frozen-g
counterfactual **CONFIRMED** that `g_frac(h)` carries the *whole* residual 2D displacement
(0.780/0.800 → 0.660/0.640; ledger #91) **[DOC]**, and `g_frac` is a **completion-leg** mass factor
(`bayesian_statistics.py:4197`), living in the 93%-weight leg that contains no galaxy sum (C-2).
P2 cannot act there. For P2 to own the 2D displacement it would have to act through the 7%-weight
catalogue leg that is *identically zero for 61.8% of events*. [INFER]
*Refute by:* stratify the 2D displacement by C-4's overlap classes — if the +0.05–0.07 is carried
disproportionately by the 385 events with a `d_L`-compatible sky-overlapping partner, H-2 revives.
This is a free CSV read (see §6, M-2).

**H-3 (P1 / the shared precomputed selection objects) — UNDETERMINED, and the live one.**
P1 is the statement with no shared-host precondition, and C-6 shows we factor out exactly the class
of object it warns about. The paper's escape clause is a **large-volume** argument. Our `L_comp` leg
satisfies it by construction (uniform-in-comoving completion density). Our `L_cat` leg, via the
pool-fed `f_k` (C-7), applies a globally-averaged completeness inside a *ball-sized* volume, where
the escape clause's premise ("the average galaxy redshift will be approximately uniform in comoving
volume") is exactly what a candidate ball is not. **Sign: undetermined** — P1 is a statement about
separability breaking, not about a direction; the paper never measures its residual.
*Refute by:* show `f_k`'s entry into `L_cat` is h-flat (then it cannot tilt the posterior at all),
or show its h-slope is bounded well below the observed displacement. The D1 arms already moved the
pool; the missing read is the **h-slope**, not the level (see §6, M-3).

**H-4 (Inconsistency-5 low-H_0 overdensity mechanism) — DISTINGUISHABLE, wrong geometry.**
[LIT] §4.2's low-H_0 bias needs *multiple events along one narrow line of sight* through a low-z
overdensity — the paper explicitly advises "against using a small area light cone simulation".
Our events are full-sky with median NN separation 2.44° and median ball radius 1.22° (C-4); we are
structurally the *opposite* of a light-cone simulation. The 279 `d_L`-compatible overlapping pairs
are the only candidates, and they are 0.022% of pairs. [INFER]
*Refute by:* M-2's stratification (same read as H-2) — if the 385 touched events pull the 1D
posterior low relative to the 1205 untouched ones, H-4 revives.

**H-5 (catalogue-realization / shared-estimator correlations) — OUT OF SCOPE OF THIS PAPER.**
The paper's only cross-event coupling is via **shared latent true redshifts** {z_g}. Correlations
induced by a shared *injection-pool estimate* of the selection functions (C-6) are structurally
similar but are **not** the Gair correlation, and the paper says nothing about them (§1, negative
results). Do not cite Gair et al. 2023 as authority for or against them.
*Refute by:* n/a — this is a scoping statement about the literature, falsified only by locating such
a statement in the paper (searched; absent).

**H-6 (adjacent canon might state it differently) — UNVERIFIED.**
Gray et al. 2020 (1908.06050) appendices, Mandel/Farr/Gair 2019 (1809.02063), and the gwcosmo
methods papers were **not** re-read this session. No claim is made about them.
*Refute by:* read them. Low priority — S1 and S2 are both fully present in 2212.08694.

