## 5. Final assessment — the author's three-way question

> *candidate for the remaining bias / already tried / unrelated?*

**Split answer. The three hypotheses do not share a verdict, and forcing one would be dishonest.**

**(a) As the owner of the 1D low rail (0.600, both venues): ~~UNRELATED — refuted, pending one check.~~**

> **[LOCAL, RESCINDED 2026-08-05 — M-1 verdict KERNEL-FINITE.]** The "single thing that would
> overturn this is M-1" fired. M-1 ran (`m1_kernel_delta_check.py` /
> `m1_kernel_delta_check.json`, this directory): `resolve_host_z_kernel`
> (`bayesian_statistics.py:149-210`) has `volume_deconv` pinned explicitly in iiib's own
> `run_metadata_0.json` (`host_z_kernel: "volume_deconv"`, `observed_catalogue: null`) —
> the point/δ branch at `:192-196` is unreachable from that config. The per-galaxy width
> input is likewise non-δ: the parent (22.6M-row) catalogue's `REDSHIFT_MEASUREMENT_ERROR`
> column has minimum 5.24e-4, median 0.0396, **0.0% exactly zero** — a parse-time
> peculiar-velocity floor (`galaxy_catalogue/handler.py:434-479`) is folded into every row
> regardless of realization. Median σ_z/(1+z) = **0.0330**, i.e. **0.94×** the GLADE
> photo-z scale (0.035) used at the joint venue, and **1.1–11×** the paper's §3.3
> demonstrated-bias range (δz/z = 0.3–3%). **Both the kernel choice and its width input
> are non-δ at iiib.** The premise of (a) — "a term that is exactly zero in a venue cannot
> own a rail in that venue" — does not hold: the term is not zero at iiib. **H-1 (the 1D
> rail via P2/P3 cross-terms) is therefore LIVE, not refuted, and is promoted to
> §6-cross-term-instrument status below.** This does not by itself say H-1 *is* the rail's
> owner — that requires the measurement in the new §"cross-term instrument" — only that the
> refutation-by-perfect-z argument is unavailable as an idealized-venue statement was
> conflated with an idealized-*kernel* statement. See the new "Author mandate" section: this
> rescission exists because the mandate forbids treating an escape clause as closed without
> checking it.

*(Original text, superseded by the above, retained for audit trail:)* ~~The paper states
twice, unambiguously, that the correction vanishes identically at perfect galaxy redshifts.
Our exact-z idealized venue rails at 0.600 just as hard as the observed venue **[LOCAL,
C-5]**. A term that is exactly zero in a venue cannot own a rail in that venue. The magnitude
argument agrees independently (7.1% mixture weight × ≤24.2% of events × 1/N_shared). *The
single thing that would overturn this is M-1.*~~

**(b) As the owner of the +0.05–0.07 2D displacement: UNRELATED — wrong leg.**
The carrier is already CONFIRMED to be `g_frac(h)` in the completion leg (ledger #91), which
contains no galaxy sum; the catalogue leg that P2 lives in carries 7.1% weight and is identically
zero for 61.8% of 2D events **[LOCAL, C-3]**. The author's S2 intuition — that mass conditioning
shrinks the host count into the paper's danger zone — is **directionally reversed**: it shrinks the
number of *shared* candidates, which is the quantity the paper's correction actually scales with.
Sign would have been right (HIGH); geometry and weight are wrong.

**(c) As a live candidate via P1 — the statement the author did NOT recall: UNDETERMINED.**
This is the finding of this intake. P1 needs **no shared host and no second event**; it says a
selection denominator that depends on the latent galaxy field may only be factored out under a
**large-volume** argument. We factor out seven such objects, once, before the event loop **[LOCAL,
C-6]** — and one of them (`f_k`) is applied inside a *ball-sized* volume where the escape clause's
premise fails **[DOC, C-7]**. This is not a new thread: **it is open thread §4 item 15**, and this
intake supplies it with a literature warrant it did not have. Sign and size: **not determined by
anything read or measured here** — P1 is a separability statement, and the paper never quantifies
its residual. UNDETERMINED is the correct answer, not a hedge.

**Meta-finding (recommend recording regardless of the above):** our calibration harness is
one-candidate-per-event and therefore **structurally blind to this entire class**, exactly as
[A3](iii) and ledger row 86 already warn. Whatever the verdict on (a)–(c), the harness gap is real
and the paper is the clean external citation for *why* it matters. This strengthens the existing
RUNBOOK-8 §1 item 7 build request rather than adding a new one.

