## Author mandate (2026-08-05)

**Binding, verbatim, applies to every step of this claim's remaining life:**

> "We need to measure the coupling terms suppressed by 1/N_gal to have an informed decision
> — either show it can be neglected or that it needs to be regarded. Do not refute it because
> it doesn't suit the current picture; that might be mathematically/scientifically just wrong."

Consequences for this file, stated explicitly so they are checkable:
1. §5(a)'s original refutation was conditional on M-1 by its own last sentence ("*the single
   thing that would overturn this is M-1*"). M-1 fired the not-null branch. The refutation is
   struck, not softened — a struck claim that turns out right after re-measurement is fine; a
   struck claim left standing on a false premise is not.
2. No further step in this claim's life may close H-1 (or H-3) by argument alone. The only
   exit from LIVE is a **measurement** against a **pre-registered** threshold (see below) — not
   a magnitude estimate, not an "unlikely because", not a re-reading of the same CSVs that
   produced C-1..C-7.
3. "NEGLECT" and "REGARD" are both legitimate outcomes of measuring. The mandate does not
   pre-judge the answer; it forbids answering by convenience. A measured, sub-threshold
   cross-term is exactly as much a discharge of the mandate as a measured, threshold-crossing
   one — the difference is what happens next (§ below).

